@page "/Error"
@layout Fravashi.Web.Components.Layout.MinimalWindowLayout
@using Fravashi.Web.Components.Shell

@* Backlog 281: the studio's own Error page. Reached only by StudioError.UseStudioError re-executing a thrown
   page request here (Services/StudioError.cs): its guard sends a DIRECT request for this route home instead,
   so the gate exemption below only ever reaches this page by way of a real thrown exception. Says only that
   something went wrong on the studio's side; the exception itself is never shown here, only logged, under the
   data directory's logs.
   <para>
   Deliberately NOT MainLayout (owner correction, 2026-10-10): this page is exempted from every redirecting gate
   so it can render instead of looping back to the page that threw, and the app is one persistent interactive
   circuit, so MainLayout's activity rail and sidebar would let a person navigate anywhere they link to on that
   SAME circuit with no new HTTP request, which is the one thing a gate-exempt page must never offer. See
   Components/Layout/MinimalWindowLayout.razor, including its own residual (the title bar's workspace and branch
   switchers) and its ErrorBoundary around WindowTitleBar.
   </para>
   <para>
   On "no circuit": a component reached through UseExceptionHandler's re-execution renders statically and never
   starts an interactive circuit, because the request still carries IExceptionHandlerPathFeature when the
   component endpoint decides how to render it. A statically rendered component emits no event handler, so the
   Home link below is a plain anchor. <c>data-enhance-nav="false"</c> keeps blazor.web.js from fetch-and-patching
   this already-at-a-500 page into the DOM on click; it does NOT change which middleware the click's request
   runs through either way, since an enhanced navigation is an ordinary HTTP GET through the same pipeline
   (gates included) as any other request. The attribute is about the browser doing a normal full navigation
   instead of an in-place DOM patch, not about which gates run.
   </para> *@

<PageTitle>@(ProductName.Short) · Something went wrong</PageTitle>
<PageBar Title="Something went wrong" />

<div class="studio-page" data-testid="studio-error">
    <p role="alert">Sorry, something went wrong on the studio's side. The details are in the studio log, under the data directory's logs.</p>
    <p><a href="/" data-testid="studio-error-home" data-enhance-nav="false">Go to the start page</a></p>
</div>
